###########################
#  Main text

BEGIN_TEXT
Simple regular parametrizations are brilliant because of the following theorem. $BR
\( \textbf{Theorem A.} \) 	If a map \( G : U \to \mathbb{R}^3 \) is a simple regular parametrization of a set \( S \subseteq \mathbb{R}^3 \), then  \( S \) is a 2-dimensional smooth manifold at \( G(c) \) for every \( c \in U^{\mathrm{o}} \). $BR $BR

In other words, these can be used to prove that a set is locally the graph of a 2-variable function.  By analyzing some incorrect arguments, you will discover some intricacies with Theorem A.

$BR 
1. Semeon attempts to incorrectly prove that a helix in \( \mathbb{R}^3 \) is not a 2-dimensional smooth manifold.
$BR
\{ image( "l1-q3-semeon-proof.png", width=>835, height=>136 ) \}

$BR
Aside from missing details, Semeon makes a mistake in one line. Identify the line.
END_TEXT

BEGIN_PGML
[@ $radio1->buttons() @]*
END_PGML

BEGIN_TEXT
$BR 
2. Fabian attempts to show that the half cone in \(  \mathbb{R}^3 \) is a 2-dimensional smooth manifold. $BR
\{ image( "l1-q3-fabian-proof.png", width=>835, height=>207 ) \}
$BR

Aside from missing details, Fabian makes a subtle mistake in one line. Identify the line.
END_TEXT

